", "notes": ["Incumbent Jim Pillen (R) is running."]}, "overview": "Nebraska's governor race pairs incumbent Republican Jim Pillen against Democratic state senator Lynne Walz, with former state senator Brett Lindstrom running as a third-party candidate. The state's Republican lean and Pillen's incumbency give him a structural advantage, and most expert raters place the race in Likely R territory, though Cook Political Report moved it to Lean R in late October.\n\nRecent polling is unusually tight, with several surveys showing the candidates within a point or two and one showing Walz narrowly ahead, though many polls carry partisan sponsors. Prediction markets still price Pillen as a strong favorite, around 83 to 85 percent. The forecast lands on a modest Pillen lead with meaningful uncertainty, keeping the race in Likely R but closer than the state's typical margins.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.0219, "margin": -11.34}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.0357, "margin": -11.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.0267, "margin": -11.0}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.0322, "margin": -11.0}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.0312, "margin": -11.5}, {"date": "2026-09-27", "p_dem": 0.0585, "margin": -10.0}, {"date": "2026-09-28", "p_dem": 0.0639, "margin": -10.0}, {"date": "2026-09-29", "p_dem": 0.0714, "margin": -9.0}, {"date": "2026-09-30", "p_dem": 0.0694, "margin": -8.5}, {"date": "2026-10-01", "p_dem": 0.0605, "margin": -9.0}, {"date": "2026-10-02", "p_dem": 0.0982, "margin": -8.5}, {"date": "2026-10-03", "p_dem": 0.0987, "margin": -8.0}, {"date": "2026-10-04", "p_dem": 0.0922, "margin": -7.5}, {"date": "2026-10-05", "p_dem": 0.0698, "margin": -8.0}, {"date": "2026-10-06", "p_dem": 0.052, "margin": -8.5}, {"date": "2026-10-07", "p_dem": 0.0503, "margin": -8.5}, {"date": "2026-10-08", "p_dem": 0.087, "margin": -7.0}, {"date": "2026-10-09", "p_dem": 0.0944, "margin": -7.0}, {"date": "2026-10-10", "p_dem": 0.1091, "margin": -6.5}, {"date": "2026-10